From 3a83e8bc4903a1629bfb4c6eb9c9e539242a5c45 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mocoy <judsonwebdesign@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 09:36:03 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc3: allow runtime PM for host-mode controllers

dwc3_core_probe() enables runtime PM with a 5 s autosuspend delay and
then immediately calls pm_runtime_forbid(), which is never lifted for
host mode - the controller stays awake permanently and, through its
power domains and bandwidth votes, keeps the whole USB subsystem (and
everything downstream of it) out of low-power states.

The runtime callbacks (dwc3_runtime_suspend/resume) fully exist and
already handle state save/restore and PHY coordination through the
glue drivers, so host-mode controllers can safely autosuspend. Drop
the forbid once the role is known: host mode gets pm_runtime_allow(),
gadget mode keeps it until its UDC lifecycle is runtime-PM aware.

Experiment-verified on X1E80100 (Yoga Slim 7x) via sysfs
pm_runtime_allow on the glue devices before this commit; kernel-side
verification pending.
---
 drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c b/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c
index ceb49f2f8..7884421bc 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c
@@ -2348,6 +2348,18 @@ int dwc3_core_probe(const struct dwc3_probe_data *data)
 			goto err_exit_debugfs;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Host-mode controllers can safely runtime-suspend: the runtime
+	 * callbacks save/restore controller state and coordinate the PHYs
+	 * via the glue drivers, and wakeup interrupts keep device
+	 * attach/removal alive. Drop the probe-time forbid so the
+	 * controller autosuspends after the configured idle delay; gadget
+	 * mode keeps the forbid until its UDC lifecycle is runtime-PM
+	 * aware.
+	 */
+	if (dwc->current_dr_role == DWC3_GCTL_PRTCAP_HOST)
+		pm_runtime_allow(dev);
+
 	pm_runtime_put(dev);
 
 	dma_set_max_seg_size(dev, UINT_MAX);
-- 
